    clflush_cache_range(entries, VTD_PAGE_SIZE);
    clflush_cache_range(&dir[dir_index].val, sizeof(*dir));
}
```
Reason: The PASID table entries are flushed *after* the directory entry has been updated to point to them. This creates a race condition: non-coherent IOMMU hardware may read the stale, uninitialized data from RAM (since the zero-initialized table may still be in CPU cache) before the cache flush writes it to memory.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+ if (!ecap_coherent(info->iommu->ecap))
+    clflush_cache_range(entries, VTD_PAGE_SIZE);
if (!try_cmpxchg64(&dir[dir_index].val, &tmp, (u64)virt_to_phys(entries) | PASID_PTE_PRESENT)) {
    iommu_free_pages(entries);
    goto retry;
}
if (!ecap_coherent(info->iommu->ecap))
    clflush_cache_range(&dir[dir_index].val, sizeof(*dir));
```
Reason: The flush of the PASID table is moved *before* the update of the directory entry. Now the zero-initialized table is written to RAM and the CPU cache is flushed before it becomes visible to non-coherent IOMMU hardware, eliminating the race condition. The subsequent flush of the directory entry itself is kept to ensure consistency of the pointer.

